# sanctioned-references — cross-repository references this public corpus is
# knowingly allowed to contain.
#
# One `owner/name` per line; `#` introduces a comment.
#
# This list does NOT assert that a reference is harmless. Nothing mechanical can
# decide that: naming a private repository's existence can be perfectly fine,
# while describing its contents would be a disclosure, and no pattern separates
# the two. What the check asks is only whether a reference is NEW. Anything not
# listed here stops the build, so the judgement is made by the person adding the
# mention, in the change that adds it, while the intent is still in front of
# them. That is the whole mechanism.
#
# Adding a line is therefore a decision, not a formality. If you are listing a
# reference to make a red build go away, that is the moment the check exists for.
#
# Listing an owner also arms its namespace: because Internal/Skills is here,
# any OTHER Internal/<name> reference will now be reported.
#
# What is watched, stated at its true size rather than a flattering one:
# references under any swift-* or rule-* namespace, which covers 54 of the
# fleet's 59 organisation namespaces, plus any owner already listed here. The
# remaining five are unrelated ventures and a personal account, and they are
# NOT watched and cannot be -- a public file naming them would be the
# disclosure it is meant to guard against. Whether a watched reference actually
# discloses anything is still not judged by anything mechanical; that part is
# unenforced, and the person who catches it is whoever writes the sentence.

# Public sibling repositories in the Institute ecosystem.
swift-institute/.github
swift-institute/Experiments
swift-institute/Issues
swift-institute/Research
swift-institute/Workspace
swift-institute/swift-institute.org

